WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:03.336
In this lesson, we'll explain
the relationship between external memory

2
00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:04.337
and RAG.

3
00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.573
We'll also implement
agentic RAG by copying

4
00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:08.908
data directly into the agent's archival memory.

5
00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:10.343
Let's get coding.

6
00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:14.047
We learned in previous lessons
that MemGPT agents can use their external memory.

7
00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:17.050
So both recall an archival memory
for agentic RAG.

8
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:20.186
So this basically means retrieving
information from archival

9
00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:23.156
and recall to inform the new messages
that it generates.

10
00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:26.126
Unlike traditional RAG,
agentic RAG allows agents

11
00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:29.129
to define both when
and how to retrieve the data.

12
00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:32.432
So, for example, with archival memory
search, it also decides

13
00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.435
what the actual query
into the search function should be.

14
00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:39.873
So you'll often see variations in what
the actual query that the agent makes is.

15
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.309
So for example here,
it shows the query dog.

16
00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:44.244
In MemGPT,

17
00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.413
agents have a general purpose
archival memory

18
00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:49.149
that is the default external memory
for the agent.

19
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:53.620
So data can be saved in here by both
the agent and the user to be used for RAG.

20
00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:57.590
So for example, rather than the agent
explicitly storing memories,

21
00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:01.661
you as a user can do things
like upload files like a handbook PDF

22
00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.630
into the archival memory.

23
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.633
Agents can also have additional forms
of external memory

24
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:09.669
or retrieve sources for RAG using tools.

25
00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.005
In this lab,
we're going to implement an agent

26
00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.908
that has access to additional tools
for RAG.

27
00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:18.244
We're going to go over an example
of accessing a fake external database.

28
00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:21.247
So once again, we're going to start

29
00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:24.884
with importing in our Letta client
and initializing the client.

30
00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:29.155
And then also using the same helper
function that we did before.

31
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.026
So in this lab we're going to go over
how to implement

32
00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:37.297
agentic RAG in Letta or basically agents
which can connect to external data

33
00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:40.533
sources. In Letta there's two different ways
you can do this.

34
00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:43.570
One is to copy data into archival memory.

35
00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.439
We saw before
how you could actually insert

36
00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:48.475
memories yourself as a developer

37
00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:50.710
into the agent's
archival memory for the agent to later

38
00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:51.911
access.

39
00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.079
Another way to do it is to

40
00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:55.648
connect the agent to external data
through a tool.

41
00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.717
So for example,
if you have your own RAG

42
00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:59.385
database, you can create a tool

43
00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.321
that can also access keys.

44
00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:03.490
To basically query
that data from your agent.

45
00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:06.559
So we're going to go over both approaches

46
00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.128
today.

47
00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:08.561
So, in

48
00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.630
Letta we have a notion of a data source.

49
00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:14.767
This is basically a group of embedded
data, or set of passages.

50
00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.337
That we can copy into agents.

51
00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:20.607
So I'm going to create a source
by creating a source

52
00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:22.876
called employee handbook,
which basically has

53
00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:25.445
the embedding model from OpenAI associated with it.

54
00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:27.313
So a source has a single

55
00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:30.850
embedding model with it because
we can't have inconsistent embeddings,

56
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.853
within a single source
or within a single agent's archival memory.

57
00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:36.156
So I've now

58
00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:37.757
created that source.

59
00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:41.161
And then I'm now actually
going to upload a file into this source.

60
00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:45.465
So, to do this is I can upload this file.

61
00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.367
To a specified source ID. And then we

62
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:50.503
have a local file
called Handbook dot pdf, which

63
00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:51.838
we're going upload.

64
00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:52.906
This upload is basically

65
00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:55.275
going to return back a job
which will have

66
00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:58.011
a certain status associated with it.

67
00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:01.014
So right now
this job has the status created.

68
00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:02.749
But we can actually

69
00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:04.884
sort of pull this job

70
00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.587
to basically see updates over time.

71
00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:11.090
So to pull this job,
we can have a while loop that checks

72
00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:13.159
for the job status to be completed.

73
00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:16.329
So here we actually have this while loop
that's checking for the status each

74
00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:18.865
time and printing it
and then sleeping for a second

75
00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:20.366
After retrieving the job.

76
00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:23.703
So here it actually happened to be
that it just completed right now.

77
00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:27.140
But you'll see also like
a running status like it's completed

78
00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:30.143
if your job is taking
a bit of longer time.

79
00:03:30.243 --> 00:03:30.877
And then once

80
00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.880
the job is completed, we can actually also
print out, the job metadata.

81
00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:34.581
So this will have

82
00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:36.049
information like the number

83
00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.518
of passages
that were derived from this job.

84
00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:41.454
So here we got
11 passages from this document

85
00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.456
and then under number of documents
associated.

86
00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:46.059
So in this case it was just one document.

87
00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:46.726
And we can also

88
00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:48.795
actually list the passages
inside of the source.

89
00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:50.997
So this will actually
have the embedding as well.

90
00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:53.099
So I'm not going to print out
the whole set of passages.

91
00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:55.835
But you can look into the passages text.

92
00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:57.237
Or other fields if you're interested.

93
00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:58.571
But yeah, you can see here

94
00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:01.874
we list out the source passages
and basically 11 passages back.

95
00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.210
So now let's actually just

96
00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.511
create an agent.

97
00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:07.747
In creating this agent,
we're going to need to make sure

98
00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.183
that the embedding model
is the same as the embedding model

99
00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:11.150
for the source.

100
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.153
Otherwise, we're going to have issues
if we try to connect the two together.

101
00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:17.257
So we create this agent

102
00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:19.726
and then now we actually want to

103
00:04:19.726 --> 00:04:21.928
attach the source to the agent.

104
00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:23.696
So we can do this by basically calling

105
00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:26.332
the attach function, and specifying the

106
00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:27.333
source ID that we want to

107
00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.502
attach,
and the agent ID that we want to attach

108
00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:30.737
it to.

109
00:04:30.737 --> 00:04:31.738
And so now we can actually

110
00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:34.474
list all the sources that have
been attached to an agent.

111
00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:37.377
And so you can see that the source
that we just created

112
00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:39.045
with its ID and then also details

113
00:04:39.045 --> 00:04:42.081
about its embedding
config, provided back into this list.

114
00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:44.984
We can also list the passages

115
00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.085
from the agent.

116
00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:47.220
So you'll see here

117
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.189
that the agent now has 11 passages
and it's archival memory.

118
00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.091
Because the passages from the source were

119
00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:55.094
copied into the agent's archival memory.

120
00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:57.563
So now we can message the agent
and ask it to

121
00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:59.866
reference data its archival memory
from this

122
00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:00.700
source.

123
00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.302
So this source was an employee

124
00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:03.870
handbook, and we can tell the agent

125
00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.238
search archival for

126
00:05:05.238 --> 00:05:08.041
the company's vacation policies.

127
00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:09.342
So we can see that the

128
00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:13.313
agent called archival memory search,
with the query vacation policies

129
00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.949
And this returned back a lot of responses.

130
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:18.184
You can basically see the

131
00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.453
content that was being returned.

132
00:05:20.453 --> 00:05:23.089
And this is return
back into the context window.

133
00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:27.026
And then the agent pulls out data
from this. And returns back

134
00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:27.860
with this message

135
00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.963
which says: "Vacations are permitted
only under the following conditions.

136
00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:34.467
You must provide an AI agent that matches
or surpasses your own competencies,

137
00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:36.769
to perform your duties
during your absence."

138
00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:37.937
So this company is very

139
00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:39.672
strict about their

140
00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:41.307
vacation policies unfortunately.

141
00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:45.011
So another way that we can connect
an agent to external

142
00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.046
data is through tools.

143
00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:49.982
So oftentimes,
you might have your own database.

144
00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:52.085
Maybe a RAG database,
or maybe some internal

145
00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:56.055
search tool
that allows both you or your agents to

146
00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:58.858
search some information that you have.

147
00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:01.728
So to show an example of this,
we can create an agent

148
00:06:01.728 --> 00:06:04.731
that has a custom tool that allows it to

149
00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:07.800
search some fake external database.

150
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.670
So to do this we're going to create,
basically a dummy tool

151
00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:13.005
that queries into a birthday database.

152
00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:13.940
We're going to give it a name.

153
00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:18.244
It'll look up the provided names
birthday by looking at this fake database.

154
00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:22.181
So, in this case, our
our database is just a dictionary but

155
00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:22.815
you can imagine

156
00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:25.251
that this could be a real database
where you do a real lookup.

157
00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:26.919
And so we

158
00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:29.922
will create this tool as birthday tool.

159
00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:31.491
And now we can create an agent that

160
00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:33.459
has access to this tool.

161
00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:34.193
So to do this

162
00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:35.928
we're going to create an agent that has

163
00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:38.898
human memory block.
The human's name is Sarah.

164
00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:42.335
And then also persona memory block
will give it some extra instructions,

165
00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:43.236
telling it you are an

166
00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:45.705
agent with access to a birthday db tool.

167
00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:46.806
You can look up information

168
00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:49.075
about user's birthday using this tool.

169
00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:52.678
And then we'll also specify
the model name, the embedding name,

170
00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:54.747
and then also pass in the birthday
tool ID.

171
00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:57.483
One other thing that you can do

172
00:06:57.483 --> 00:06:59.152
if you are actually connecting to a real

173
00:06:59.152 --> 00:07:02.221
database, is you can actually specify per

174
00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:05.224
agent tool execution environment
variables.

175
00:07:05.224 --> 00:07:06.092
So oftentimes

176
00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:10.663
with a database or any kind of search API,
you have API keys or secrets that

177
00:07:10.663 --> 00:07:11.798
you need to pass in.

178
00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:14.801
And so if you pass in this variable
you can

179
00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:17.570
send things into the agent's tool
execution environment

180
00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.640
that can have access
to this kind of data. So,

181
00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:23.476
obviously since we have a fake database,
we don't actually need this.

182
00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:25.611
But if you are connecting
to a real database,

183
00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:26.779
this might be something
that you want to do.

184
00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:28.781
So now we can actually

185
00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:31.918
message the agent and ask it
when is my birthday?

186
00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:34.854
And it should be able to figure out,
using its tool.

187
00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:37.590
What my birthday actually is.

188
00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:38.825
So we can see that the

189
00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:40.493
agent is reasoning, and it

190
00:07:40.493 --> 00:07:43.129
calls the query birthday database tool.

191
00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:44.797
And request heartbeat equals true.

192
00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:46.365
It gets back a birthday.

193
00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:47.467
And calls

194
00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:49.469
the send message tool
to say your birthday is

195
00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:52.238
July 6th, 1993.

196
00:07:52.238 --> 00:07:53.873
So this was obviously a very simple dummy

197
00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:55.408
example, but you can imagine

198
00:07:55.408 --> 00:07:56.943
how you could write custom database

199
00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:58.544
connectors or custom queries.

200
00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:00.813
And connect them to your agent.

201
00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:05.518
So congratulations, you've now learned
how to connect your agent to custom

202
00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:08.354
external data sources,
both through the data sources

203
00:08:08.354 --> 00:08:10.223
concept inside of Letta and

204
00:08:10.223 --> 00:08:12.291
also through defining custom tools.

205
00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:14.694
We hope that you can figure out
how to add

206
00:08:14.694 --> 00:08:16.262
interesting data sources to your agent

207
00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:19.599
to make sure your agent has
as much context and information as

208
00:08:19.599 --> 00:08:20.066
possible.

